unarchive the modern secure way, and let a subclass through

iA Writer TERMINATED, 3 runs of 3, on

  +[NSKeyedUnarchiver unarchivedObjectOfClasses:fromData:error:]: unrecognized selector

Kit.framework calls it and nothing implemented it. The singular form was worse than missing: the
header DECLARED +unarchivedObjectOfClass:fromData:error: and no implementation existed, so a caller
that compiled against it got an unrecognized selector at run time.

TWO DEFECTS, and the second would have bitten the moment the first was fixed.

1  The modern form ANSWERS AN ERROR where the old one raises, and it turns secure coding on whether
   or not the caller asks. Both forms are here now; the singular one is the plural one with a one
   element set. An exception during the decode becomes NSCoderReadCorruptError with the reason in
   NSDebugDescriptionErrorKey rather than escaping to the caller.

2  A SUBCLASS OF AN ALLOWED CLASS IS ALLOWED. Both secure coding checks asked
   [allowedClassSet containsObject:class], which is an exact match, so an archive naming
   NSMutableArray against an allowed NSArray was rejected. Darwin allows it. classIsAllowed asks
   isSubclassOfClass: over the set. Turning secure coding on for the new methods without this would
   have failed every non exact archive.

The five NSCoder error codes were missing from FoundationErrors.h. 4864 was already in this file as
a bare literal in a userInfo dictionary, which is what it is called.

MEASURED, iA Writer through scripts/app-rate.sh, same configuration both times

  before  3 terminations in 3 runs, all on that selector
  after   0 in 6, and 6 of 6 survived to the time limit

WHAT DID NOT CHANGE. The library rows still say Updating... and the editor is still empty. This
removes a crash that was killing the run outright; it is not the end of #194.
